          "text": "## Review: Crucible — Multi-Agent Debate System\n\nI've read through the entire codebase. Here's my honest assessment.\n\n---\n\n### What It Does Well\n\n**1. Structured adversarial pressure is genuinely valuable.**\nThe core insight — that a single LLM call often produces plausible-sounding but subtly wrong answers, while forcing agents to attack each other surfaces real flaws — is well-supported by research. The PROPOSE → SELF_CRITIQUE → CRITIQUE → DEFEND pipeline is a sound implementation of this.\n\n**2. Personas are a smart debiasing mechanism.**\nSince all debaters share the same underlying model, they'd naturally converge on the same blind spots. Assigning distinct lenses (Correctness-First, Simplicity-First, Devil's Advocate, Pragmatist, Innovator) forces genuinely different trade-off reasoning, not just cosmetic variation. This is one of the strongest design choices.\n\n**3. Progressive summarization is practical.**\nFeeding the arbiter full text for Round 1 and the final round, but only digests for intermediate rounds, is a pragmatic solution to context window limits without losing the debate arc.\n\n**4. The severity taxonomy ([FATAL], [MAJOR], [MINOR]) adds rigor.**\nIt forces agents to triage rather than dump a flat list of objections. This channels debate energy toward what matters.\n\n**5. Shared-bias detection in the arbiter is a critical feature.**\nThe explicit instruction to watch for \"unanimous agreement never actually tested\" and to independently verify with tools addresses the fundamental weakness of same-model debates.\n\n**6. Adaptive rounds with focus areas.**\nRather than re-debating everything, narrowing subsequent rounds to specific unresolved disagreements makes later rounds more productive.\n\n---\n\n### Weaknesses and Concerns\n\n**1. Evidence gathering is too constrained for deep correctness.**\nAgents get 2-4 tool calls per phase. For genuinely complex technical questions (e.g., \"is this thread-safe?\"), you often need to read multiple source files, check documentation, and verify edge cases. 2-4 calls may force agents to argue from model knowledge rather than verified facts — which is exactly what the system is trying to avoid.\n\n**2. Convergence detection is a weak point.**\nUsing a single Sonnet call to determine if N agents have converged is fragile. \"Essentially the same solution\" is subjective and hard to classify from text. False convergence (agents using different words for genuinely different approaches) and false divergence (agents agreeing but phrasing differently) are both risks. There's no structured comparison — it's just vibes-based classification.\n\n**3. The arbiter has enormous power with limited accountability.**\nThe arbiter can override all agents, declare shared-bias overrides, and synthesize a \"best answer\" that none of the agents actually proposed. This is both a strength (it can go beyond debate limitations) and a risk (a single model call making unchecked judgment calls). There's no mechanism to verify the arbiter's reasoning.\n\n**4. Cost scaling is brutal and may limit real-world adoption.**\nWith 3 agents and 3 rounds: ~27 agent calls minimum. With 5 agents and 5 rounds: 80+. Each call is a full LLM invocation. For tasks where a single well-prompted call would get 90% of the way there, the marginal improvement from debate may not justify 20-80x the cost.\n\n**5. No mechanism for agents to request more information from the user.**\nIf the task is ambiguous, all agents will independently interpret it differently. The debate then spends rounds resolving ambiguity that a single clarifying question would have fixed. A pre-debate clarification step would help.\n\n**6. The \"SEND_BACK for one more round\" limit is arbitrary.**\nIf the arbiter detects a critical gap, limiting it to exactly one send-back is a pragmatic constraint but not principled. The arbiter might need zero or two send-backs depending on the situation.\n\n**7. Structured output 