          "text": "## Crucible Review\n\nI've read every file in the project. Here's my assessment.\n\n---\n\n### What it does well\n\n**1. The debate structure is sound.** The pipeline — propose → self-critique → cross-attack → defend → converge → arbitrate — mirrors how real adversarial review improves quality. Self-critique before cross-attack is a smart addition; it forces agents to harden positions before opponents see them, reducing low-hanging-fruit attacks that waste rounds.\n\n**2. Personas are genuinely useful.** Correctness-First, Simplicity-First, Devil's Advocate, Pragmatist, Innovator — these aren't cosmetic labels. They force different trade-off weightings, which is the main thing same-model debates struggle with. Without personas, 2–5 Sonnet instances tend to converge on the same \"safe\" answer immediately. The debater prompt reinforces this well: *\"Two agents with different personas given the same task should produce genuinely different solutions — not cosmetic variations of the same idea.\"*\n\n**3. The arbiter design is strong.** Several things stand out:\n- **Provenance Map** (`[AGREED]`, `[CONTESTED → ruling]`, `[ARBITER ADDITION]`, `[SHARED-BIAS OVERRIDE]`) — this is the single best feature. It forces traceability, which prevents the arbiter from hallucinating novel content that no agent actually argued for.\n- **Shared-bias check** — explicitly calling out that all agents share the same training data and may all be wrong in the same way. This is a real problem with same-model debates and most frameworks ignore it.\n- **Send-back mechanism** — letting the arbiter request one more targeted round rather than making a premature ruling is a good escape valve.\n- **Tool access for verification** — the arbiter can independently fact-check agent claims with `Read`, `Grep`, `Glob`.\n\n**4. Progressive summarization** is necessary and well-designed. Feeding full transcripts of every round to the arbiter would blow context. Keeping Round 1 proposals in