 a worse final answer. If it falsely says DIVERGED, you waste an expensive round. There's no calibration or confidence threshold — just a single word from a single model call.\n\n**4. Digests lose information.** The progressive summarization extracts `### What Changed` and `### Round Digest` sections, but these are self-reported by the agents themselves. An agent might downplay a concession in its digest or omit a weakness it didn't fix. The arbiter is making final decisions partially based on agents' self-summaries of their own performance — a conflict of interest. Consider having the convergence-check agent or a separate summarizer produce the digests instead.\n\n**5. No mechanism for introducing external ground truth.** Debaters can use `WebSearch`, `Read`, `Grep`, `Glob` — but there's no structured step where evidence is pooled and verified before the debate continues. Agent A might cite evidence that Agent B never sees (it's buried in A's proposal text). A shared evidence board that all agents can reference would strengthen the epistemic quality.\n\n**6. The debater prompt asks for both rigorous output format AND creative thinking.** The structured output format (headers, sections, severity tags) is good for the orchestrator to parse, but it adds cognitive load to the debater. If the model spends tokens formatting, it has fewer tokens for actual reasoning. This is a minor concern with current context sizes, but worth noting.\n\n**7. No handling of the \"agreeable agent\" failure mode.** If one agent is consistently conceding to the other (which Sonnet tends to do — it's trained to be agreeable), the debate degenerates into one agent proposing and the other rubber-stamping with minor tweaks. The personas partially mitigate this, but there's no structural detection of \"Agent X conceded everything and added nothing.\" The arbiter scorecard captures this post-hoc (Intellectual Honesty score), but by then the debate has already been wasted.\n\n---\n\n### Verdict\n\